---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { PRINCIPLES, SITE } from '../data/site';
---
<Page
  title="About"
  label="About"
  heading="A small group with a simple job"
  intro="Find out how attacks actually work, explain it clearly, and help the people defending against them."
>
  <p>
    Crackbot started as a handful of people doing security research in their spare time: reversing samples,
    chasing odd bugs, rebuilding incidents from logs. The work was good, but it lived in private notes where it
    helped nobody. We formed the group to change that, and to hold each other to a higher standard than any of us
    would manage alone.
  </p>
  <p>
    We’re independent. We don’t sell products, and no vendor decides what we publish or when. That independence only
    means something if it comes with discipline, so we have a few rules we don’t bend.
  </p>

  <h2>What we hold ourselves to</h2>
  {PRINCIPLES.map((p) => (
    <>
      <h3>{p.title}</h3>
      <p>{p.body}</p>
    </>
  ))}

  <h2>What we don’t do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>We don’t test anything without permission, including “just a quick scan”.</li>
    <li>We don’t sell or trade vulnerabilities or exploits.</li>
    <li>We don’t publish personal data, even when it turns up in a leak we’re analysing.</li>
    <li>We don’t name individuals as attackers without strong, published evidence.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Working with us</h2>
  <p>
    We’re open to collaborating with other researchers, students who want to learn by doing real work, and
    organisations that want a second pair of eyes on something. Our tools and lab notes go up on
    <a href={SITE.github} rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a> once they’re safe to share.
    If you’d like to get involved, <a href="/contact/">get in touch</a>, and tell us what you’ve been working on.
  </p>
</Page>
